WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.775
A display board shows a number in binary, lighting one panel for every 1 bit. For every number

2
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:11.392
from 0 through count, report how many panels are lit. Return an array of length count + 1

3
00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:14.567
where entry i is the number of 1 bits in the binary form of i.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.312
0 to 5 in binary are 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, 101, so the counts are 0, 1,

5
00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:24.775
1, 2, 1, 2.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.395
Only 0 is shown, with no lit panels.

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.589
Dropping the last bit gives a smaller number whose answer is already known.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.870
Create bits[0] = 0 and for i from 1 to count set bits[i] = bits[i &gt;&gt; 1] +

9
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:46.624
(i &amp; 1).

10
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.510
This is a tiny dynamic program. i &gt;&gt; 1 drops the lowest bit and is smaller than i,

11
00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:58.907
so its count is already computed, and I add back i &amp; 1. Each entry takes constant time,

12
00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:06.523
O(n) overall. An equivalent recurrence is bits[i] = bits[i &amp; (i - 1)] + 1, which clears the

13
00:01:06.523 --> 00:01:08.135
lowest set bit instead.

14
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:19.768
bits[1] = bits[0] + 1 = 1; bits[2] = bits[1] + 0 = 1; bits[3] = bits[1] +

15
00:01:19.768 --> 00:01:20.831
1 = 2.

16
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.686
bits[4] = bits[2] + 0 = 1; bits[5] = bits[2] + 1 = 2.

17
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.751
O(n). O(1) beyond the output
